The independent review finds no Critical issues, but one Important finding:

| ID | Severity | Finding | Failure scenario |
|---|---|---|---|
| I6 | Important | Unbounded threaded control server admits idle unauthenticated sockets before token verification. | A local process can open many loopback connections without sending a newline. `ThreadingMixIn` creates an unbounded daemon thread per socket; the 1-second timeout and token validation happen inside those threads. Enough sockets can exhaust threads, file descriptors, or memory and make the only safe `status`/`stop` control path unavailable while the owner retains both locks. |

The relevant source referents are:

- [`playback.py:595`](/run/media/brunner56/MyBook/Workspaces/KotorPhasor/.worktrees/lua-boundary-validation/scripts/harness/kpharness/playback.py:595) through `:612`: the handler reads before authenticating.
- [`playback.py:615`](/run/media/brunner56/MyBook/Workspaces/KotorPhasor/.worktrees/lua-boundary-validation/scripts/harness/kpharness/playback.py:615) through `:618`: unbounded `ThreadingMixIn` server.
- [`playback.py:47`](/run/media/brunner56/MyBook/Workspaces/KotorPhasor/.worktrees/lua-boundary-validation/scripts/harness/kpharness/playback.py:47) through `:50`: the idle connection occupies its handler for up to one second.

The fix should impose bounded admission before spawning the handler thread—e.g. a semaphore around `process_request_thread` that closes/rejects excess sockets—and add a regression test that exceeds the limit while proving bounded thread count plus working authenticated `status` and `stop`.

